  summary: "Until 1974 agriculture dominated Ethiopia's economy, yet a feudal tenure system of rist in the north and tenancy in the south kept the peasantry insecure, while the state taxed land but invested less than 2 percent of its budget in farming. Land reform committees from 1961 and the 1964 tenancy bill changed nothing because parliament defended the landlords, and student cries of 'Land to the Tiller' grew louder as famine came. Trade relied on coffee exports, industry stayed tiny, and the early five-year plans produced meager results.",
